<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Weihaiwei</h1><p class="st-meta">1898 CE – 1930 CE · capital Port Edward</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A British naval leased territory on the Shandong coast, a quiet backwater ruled by scholar-commissioners until its return to China in 1930.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1898, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> leased <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lushunkou">Port Arthur</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> obtained Weihaiwei from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> on a lease to last as long as Russia held Port Arthur. It served as a summer anchorage for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> Station, with a base on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liugong-island">Liugong</a> island.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After a brief military administration, a civil commissioner governed from Port Edward. The territory was never developed into a major port or base, and British rule was light, working through village headmen and Chinese custom. Its commissioners included the scholar-administrators <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-stewart-lockhart">James Stewart Lockhart</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reginald-johnston">Reginald Johnston</a>, former tutor to the last Qing emperor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puyi">Puyi</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> agreed to return Weihaiwei at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-naval-conference">Washington Conference</a> of 1922, and it was handed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nationalist-government">Nationalist government</a> on 1 October 1930, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> kept the use of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liugong-island">Liugong</a> island facilities for some years more.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/weihaiwei.jpg" alt="Weihaiwei" width="320" height="180"><figcaption>Image: Nyx Ning · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gate_of_happiness_%E5%B1%B1%E4%B8%9C_%E5%A8%81%E6%B5%B7_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1898 CE – 1930 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Port Edward</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>James Stewart Lockhart, Reginald Johnston</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese folk religion, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mandarin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>China</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British Weihaiwei, Port Edward</dd></dl></aside></div>
